&lt;p&gt;Key observations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Linear memory growth&lt;/strong&gt;: RSS consumption grew predictably from 420MB to 638MB under doubled load, with no memory leaks detected over the 14-minute test window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;p99 latency degradation&lt;/strong&gt;: At 100 concurrent users, p99 latency breached our 3000ms threshold by 5%, primarily due to database connection pool saturation (default 10 connections).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Error rate spike&lt;/strong&gt;: Failed requests jumped from 1.2% to 4.1% during the spike phase, traced to webhook timeout defaults (30s) colliding with AI gateway retries.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Production Threshold
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For workflows processing synchronous webhooks, n8n maintains acceptable performance up to approximately 50-60 concurrent executions per 4-core node before requiring horizontal scaling. The architecture becomes economically viable when:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need visual workflow building with versioning (vs. hardcoding orchestration logic)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Execution volume exceeds 100k workflows/day (amortizing self-hosting overhead)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Integrations span 10+ SaaS tools (avoiding custom OAuth/API clients)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below 20k executions/month, serverless alternatives like AWS Step Functions offer better cost efficiency. Above 500k/day, you'll need PostgreSQL read replicas and Redis-backed queue management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Configuration Hardening
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To push throughput beyond baseline:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// docker-compose.override.yml additions&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;n8n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;DB_POSTGRESDB_POOL_SIZE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;25&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;EXECUTIONS_TIMEOUT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;N8N_PAYLOAD_SIZE_MAX&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;32&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;QUEUE_BULL_REDIS_HOST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;redis&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;deploy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;resources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;limits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;memory&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2G&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Increasing the PostgreSQL pool size to 25 reduced p99 latency by 18% during spike phases. Webhook timeout extension to 60s cut error rates to 1.9% at 100 VUs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reality Check
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;n8n's strength lies in rapid integration assembly, not raw throughput. When your workflow orchestration requires sub-500ms response times or handles 200+ RPS sustained load, you're better served by FastAPI microservices with direct SDK calls. The sweet spot is complex, human-supervised automations where visual debugging and non-engineer collaboration outweigh millisecond optimizations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your AI enrichment step becomes the bottleneck (as ours did with p95 LLM latency at 840ms), consider replacing synchronous calls with async message queues. The multi-provider gateway configuration we used (&lt;code&gt;baseURL: "https://b-lost.com/v1"&lt;/code&gt;) provided automatic failover that reduced timeout-related errors by 62% compared to direct OpenAI calls, but added 15-25ms routing overhead—acceptable for batch workflows, problematic for real-time webhooks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run your own benchmarks with production-representative payload sizes and integration chains. The 4-core baseline metrics above shift dramatically if your workflows include file processing, SMTP sending, or database bulk inserts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fnwcsq4q3bcmm2i8ff8dm.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fnwcsq4q3bcmm2i8ff8dm.jpg" alt=" " width="800" height="537"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

