&lt;p&gt;Most people don't get hacked by nation-state attacks. They get hacked by a fake link in a text message, a convincing "verify your account" email, a malware attachment disguised as an invoice, or a password that already leaked in someone else's data breach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built &lt;strong&gt;Aegis&lt;/strong&gt; — one place where a non-technical person can check each of those things &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; it hurts them, built to the same security standard it checks for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;Live demo:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://aegis-cyber-defense-platform.onrender.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://aegis-cyber-defense-platform.onrender.com&lt;/a&gt; (free hosting — first load after idle can take ~ a minute)&lt;br&gt;
💻 &lt;strong&gt;Code:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/Manoj-2424150118/aegis-cyber-defense-platform" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/Manoj-2424150118/aegis-cyber-defense-platform&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Smart Scan&lt;/strong&gt; is the front door: paste anything — a link, an email, a suspicious message — and Aegis detects what it is and routes it to the right analyser automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Underneath are &lt;strong&gt;10 tools&lt;/strong&gt;, each answering a question a worried user actually asks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;URL Analyser&lt;/strong&gt; — "Is this link safe?" IP-instead-of-domain, look-alike domains, brand names hidden in paths, shorteners, credential bait.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Email Analyser&lt;/strong&gt; — "Is this email really from my bank?" SPF/DKIM/DMARC authentication, display-name mismatches, pressure language, and every link inside re-scored.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;File Scanner&lt;/strong&gt; — "Is this attachment dangerous?" Content-vs-extension mismatches, double extensions like &lt;code&gt;invoice.pdf.exe&lt;/code&gt;, hidden code. Files are examined in memory only — never opened, never run, never saved.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Password Lab&lt;/strong&gt; — offline guess-time estimation, common/repetitive pattern detection, secure generation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Breach Check&lt;/strong&gt; — k-anonymity lookup: only the first five characters of the password's hash ever leave the machine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Secret Scanner&lt;/strong&gt; — finds leaked API keys and credentials hiding in text and code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Encrypted Vault&lt;/strong&gt; — AES-256-GCM with keys derived via Argon2id; the server stores only scrambled data, and tampering is detected automatically.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Website Audit&lt;/strong&gt; — any site graded A–F on HTTPS enforcement, security headers and cookie safety.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scam Message Checker&lt;/strong&gt; — recognises Indian scam scripts: fake "digital arrest" threats, KYC/account-block warnings, OTP harvesting, Telegram task scams.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Domain &amp;amp; TLS Checker&lt;/strong&gt; — live grading of a domain's email-auth records, plus a real handshake to verify a site's certificate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The part most tools skip: the fix
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every finding in every report ends with a &lt;strong&gt;Suggested Fix&lt;/strong&gt; — concrete next steps written for the person holding the report. A test enforces that no finding can ship without one. A report should end the problem, not just name it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since v2.0, a &lt;strong&gt;detection-engine "Signal" layer&lt;/strong&gt; sits across the analysers, and public &lt;strong&gt;Trends&lt;/strong&gt; show what people are actually checking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How it protects itself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A security tool has to survive its own audit:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The website-audit feature could once have been tricked into fetching internal network addresses (SSRF). It now resolves every target first, refuses private/internal addresses, and re-checks every redirect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Uploads are read in capped chunks and rejected exactly at the 10 MB limit — no memory-exhaustion via huge files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Login responses are timing-equalised with generic errors, so attackers can't enumerate real accounts. Accounts lock after repeated failures; login is rate-limited.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Auth is Argon2id + JWT, and the platform ships the same strict security headers its own audit module grades other sites on. Aegis's self-audit score: &lt;strong&gt;100, grade A&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Testing is evidence, not decoration
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;186 automated tests&lt;/strong&gt;, all passing, with CI and CodeQL green on every push. The canonical reference scores never drift between releases: the reference phishing link scores 85, the phishing email 100, the disguised program 40 — in every battery, on every version. A scripted QA battery (functional checks, attack suite, fuzzing, load) runs in a sandbox before deployment and against the live site after it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FastAPI (Python) back end, React + TypeScript front end, single Docker container, Neon Postgres for storage (accounts and vault items survive redeploys — proven by test).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Honest limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The demo runs on free hosting, which sleeps when idle — the first load can take about a minute. The code is identical to what a paid deployment would run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The checkers use explained heuristics, not magic. They can over- or under-flag at the margins — which is exactly why every finding shows its reasoning, so the user can judge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




